# TBB low-bandwidth field profile — append to a unit's .env for a standalone
# deployment on a metered, marginal, or intermittent link (LTE, long-range wifi,
# solar site). Every setting below trades dashboard richness for bytes and power.
#
# MEASURED BASELINE (tbb-test, Pi Zero 2 W + USB mic, ~310 detections/day at
# >=0.7, mean clip 61KB). Wifi interface counters over 26h, steady state after
# subtracting maintenance traffic:
#
#   clip uploads to BirdNET-Cloud   ~19 MB/day   <-- 60% of the total
#   TLS handshakes (pre-Session)    ~9 MB/day    <-- fixed in code, not config
#   heartbeats @60s                 ~2 MB/day
#   tbb_sync keep-alives @45s        ~2 MB/day
#   detection JSON                  ~0.5 MB/day
#   DNS probes @60s                 ~0.3 MB/day
#   ------------------------------------------------
#   default profile                 ~33 MB/day  (~1.0 GB/month)
#   this profile                    ~10 MB/day  (~0.3 GB/month)
#
# POWER is NOT measurable in software on a Pi Zero 2 W — it has no PMIC ADC
# (`vcgencmd pmic_read_adc` returns "Command not registered"). Published figures
# put the board at ~0.4-0.7W headless idle and ~1.3-2W loaded; at the ~25% CPU
# this workload actually uses, expect roughly 1.0-1.5W plus ~0.1-0.25W for the
# mic, so ~1.2-1.7W / 29-41Wh per day. Do NOT size a solar rig off that estimate:
# put an inline USB power meter on the unit for 24h and use the real figure. Wifi
# retry behaviour at the edge of range can dominate everything else.

# --- Uplink ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Biggest single saving. Detections still arrive complete; only the playable
# audio is dropped. Clips are still written locally and pruned on the normal
# retention schedule, so the archive is intact for later collection.
BIRDBRAIN_BIRDNETCLOUD_UPLOAD_CLIPS=false

# Poll for new detections every 5 min instead of 1. Detections batch up and go
# in one connection, which is strictly cheaper than the same rows dribbling out
# one request at a time.
BIRDBRAIN_BIRDNETCLOUD_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# Heartbeat every 30 min. This only drives the "last seen" freshness on their
# dashboard; it has no effect on data integrity.
BIRDBRAIN_BIRDNETCLOUD_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS=1800

# Central sync: same reasoning. The high-water mark means a longer interval
# costs latency, never data.
BIRDBRAIN_TBB_SYNC_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# --- Storage --------------------------------------------------------------
# A standalone unit is likelier to be visited rarely, so keep less audio on the
# card: less SD wear, more slack before the filesystem tightens up. 388MB held
# 6505 clips at 14 days; 7 days roughly halves that.
BIRDBRAIN_TBB_CLIP_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# --- IMPORTANT: the watchdog will reboot-loop on a site with no internet -----
# birdbrain-net-watchdog reboots the unit after 20 failed DNS probes in its
# 30-probe window. That is correct for a unit that SHOULD have connectivity and
# wrong for one deployed where connectivity is intermittent or absent: it will
# reboot as often as the rate limit allows, each time costing a minute of
# recording plus a BirdNET model reload.
#
# The reboot is already rate-limited to once per 6h, so it degrades rather than
# thrashes — but for a deliberately offline or marginal site, raise the reboot
# threshold above the window so only the (harmless, cheap) wifi-cycle step runs:
#
#   sudo systemctl edit birdbrain-net-watchdog.service
#     [Service]
#     Environment=REBOOT_AT=999
#
# The unit records perfectly well with no internet at all: detections go to
# local SQLite, clips to the card, and BOTH sync targets drain from their
# high-water marks whenever connectivity returns. Nothing is lost by being
# offline for days.
